WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.302
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.302 --> 00:00:11.109
I came to next to a patch of
black ice on a cold sidewalk

00:00:11.109 --> 00:00:14.290
somewhere in downtown New Haven.

00:00:16.076 --> 00:00:22.640
I was drunk, I didn't know where I was,
and felt completely lost.

00:00:23.993 --> 00:00:29.290
The only thing I needed to do in that
moment was to sit there and start crying.

00:00:30.685 --> 00:00:35.650
Those nights were all too common for
me in my early years of college.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.809
The thing that I most
remember about that time

00:00:41.809 --> 00:00:46.376
was that I just couldn't sit with myself.

00:00:46.376 --> 00:00:47.120
I was scared.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:51.310
In addition to the normal
angst of being a teenager,

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:54.470
I was coming to terms with the fact
that I was attracted to men.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.590
I wanted things to be different.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.079
I wanted to be different.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.040
And so
I turned to whatever I could to get away.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:12.460
Alcohol, drugs, and my favorite and
most acceptable distraction, work.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:19.305
But no matter what I did,
inevitably I would come back

00:01:19.305 --> 00:01:24.187
to myself,
just as I had that night in New Haven.

00:01:24.187 --> 00:01:27.381
I woke up the next morning
with the concerned

00:01:27.381 --> 00:01:31.710
face of a nurse hovering over
me in Yale New Haven Hospital.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:38.650
When my dean caught wind of the fact that
I had drank myself into a hospital bed,

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:40.609
he immediately summoned
me into his office.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:43.060
I was terrified.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:49.380
I thought that I had blown this once in
a lifetime opportunity to go to Yale, and

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.280
that I was about to be
in a lot of trouble.

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:56.792
And yet,
when I walked into his office that day,

00:01:56.792 --> 00:02:00.105
he greeted me with a big smile and a hug.

00:02:00.105 --> 00:02:01.979
And he just started asking me questions.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.770
Today I know that he could sense the deep

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:08.250
existential angst that I
was feeling at the time.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.090
And he recommended two books for me.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:17.620
One was a Christian inspired
book called Traveling Mercies.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:21.500
The other was a Buddhist inspired
book called Yoga and the Quest for

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:22.010
the True Self.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:27.589
As I carried those two books
outside of his office with me,

00:02:27.589 --> 00:02:32.460
he quietly said, hey Vince, you might
want to check out church as well.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:37.217
Life is hard.

00:02:37.217 --> 00:02:40.692
Sometimes we find ourselves
in situations where we

00:02:40.692 --> 00:02:45.350
are absolutely overwhelmed
by the demands of reality.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:46.620
Where we can't cope.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:53.260
Where the only thing we want to
do is crawl out of our own skin,

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:55.110
or crawl underneath a rock somewhere.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:58.850
Where do we turn when
we hit the black ice?

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.620
When we fall,
how do we pick ourselves back up?

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:09.100
For many, the answer is religion, but for

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.800
me at the time,
that would have been quite a leap.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:17.160
You see, my religion during high
school was the anti-religion.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.955
I was a card carrying atheist, and I loved
to proving it to people around campus.

00:03:21.955 --> 00:03:25.846
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In fact, I remember one afternoon,

00:03:25.846 --> 00:03:29.866
I got into a fight with
three Christian friends and

00:03:29.866 --> 00:03:33.407
managed to bring all
three of them to tears,

00:03:33.407 --> 00:03:37.946
as I poked cold holes of logic
in their Christian faith.

00:03:37.946 --> 00:03:43.250
Now, I can't say that I'm proud of
the hurt that I caused my peers that day,

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:47.170
but I can say that I do
understand some of the angst

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:49.160
that I felt towards religion at the time.

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:54.800
After all, I'd been brought up
in a strict Catholic upbringing.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:59.610
And have experienced some of the most
hurtful aspects of organized religion.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.870
When I was in school,
I would get physically whipped for

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:09.260
not being able to recite pieces
of Scripture, or certain prayers.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:15.250
When the first signs of my attraction
to men started to bubble up,

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:19.900
I went under the worst type of
ridicule from my friends in school.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:25.730
And a much more subtle, though no less
hurtful, form of repression at home.

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:32.290
All grounded in the deep religious
conviction that being gay was wrong.

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:34.170
And that I was broken.

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:40.280
Yes, those tears that
afternoon in high school,

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:43.415
those were my revenge
against religious zealotry.

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:49.950
And yet, here I was,
poring again over Buddhist text and

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:54.560
Christian Scripture, and
actually starting to go to church again.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.540
You see,
the pain that I was in at the time,

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:03.480
that feeling of dislocation,
of alienation from myself

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.960
was even worse than the pain that
religion had inflicted upon me.

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:14.380
It felt as if my whole world
was just swirling around me and

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:18.100
I just needed something
steady to grab onto.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:27.340
But if I was going to go down that path
again, I was going to do it on my terms.

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:33.190
Instead of adhering to any one
type of religious system or dogma,

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:38.160
I decided to cast a wide net,
and began a spiritual journey

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:43.740
that took me from
Christianity to Zen Buddhism,

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:48.420
to Native American animism, and even
the rationalism of Enlightenment thinkers.

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:54.910
And over the course of that journey,
I became a sort of spiritual omnivore.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:58.710
Sampling different pieces and ideas and

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:04.010
concepts from these religious philosophies
in order to construct my own world view.

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:06.220
And my own sense of meaning in the world.

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:11.120
Over the course of that journey,
there were three habits

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:14.910
that I noticed that were common across
each one of these philosophies.

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:17.630
That helped me pick myself back up and

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:21.340
I hope will be helpful for you regardless
of where you are in your journey.

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:26.610
The first is finding purpose
in your experiences.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:28.530
Seeking out the meaning and

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:33.690
opportunities that can arise from even
the most challenging circumstances.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:37.250
In Traveling Mercies,
the book recommended by my dean,

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:42.690
she talks about herself as a child of God,
learning to walk on the Earth.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:47.340
And sees each relapse,
because she's a recovering alcoholic,

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:51.280
as an opportunity for her to get back up,
stronger than before.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:57.290
Now while I couldn't quite subscribe
to the idea of God the Father, and

00:06:57.290 --> 00:07:02.230
me as a child, I did like the idea
that the universe would put these

00:07:02.230 --> 00:07:07.160
trials in my way as a way of
testing me and making me stronger.

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:12.816
And so the concept of a spiritual trial
was one that I added to my spiritual

00:07:12.816 --> 00:07:13.350
plate.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:17.880
The second idea or habit that I saw

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:23.430
was developing practices that
help you honor that purpose.

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:28.614
My first yoga instructor was a woman named
Maureen, born straight out of the 60s.

00:07:28.614 --> 00:07:31.806
She was actually married to
Michael Bolton for a while.

00:07:31.806 --> 00:07:33.405
Rest in peace.

00:07:33.405 --> 00:07:35.870
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Through yoga,

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:40.990
Maureen taught me how to listen to
the language of my body and emotions, and

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:46.090
taught me the power of breath to keep
myself grounded in challenging situations.

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:49.020
Instead of running in the way
that I had in the past,

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:54.430
away from these types of stressors,
today, thanks to people like Maureen,

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:58.270
I can hold myself in these moments,
and share space,

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:01.285
even at a time like today when I'm
pretty nervous, as you can imagine.

00:08:01.285 --> 00:08:03.120
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:05.240
that was a physical practice
I added to my plate as well.

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:10.620
The final habit that I saw is
that of building community.

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:16.870
My friend Eliza and I met in a Haitian
dance class, shaking our booties,

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:20.240
which, by the way,
is another really fun spiritual practice.

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:22.180
You should try it sometime.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:28.100
And since then, Eliza and I have continued
to laugh, dance and cry together.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:31.810
Eliza is the type of spiritual
body that I wish everybody had.

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:35.930
When I fall, she helps pick me up,
when things are going all right,

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:39.390
she holds me accountable, and
isn't afraid to call me out on things.

00:08:42.806 --> 00:08:46.871
In an increasingly secular society,
with all that's going on and

00:08:46.871 --> 00:08:51.372
the increasing stress and chaos that
we find ourselves in, it's more and

00:08:51.372 --> 00:08:56.600
more important for us to know where
to turn in those moments of duress.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:59.550
And yet,
as we have an increasingly diverse and

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:04.690
wide array of spiritual philosophies,
the onus is increasingly on

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:09.769
us to make sense of our own world view and
to make our own meaning out of life.

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:17.120
Over the course of my journey,
I learned to put my faith in three habits.

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:20.583
The question that I leave with you
today is where you'll put your faith.

00:09:20.583 --> 00:09:22.525
Thank you.

00:09:22.525 --> 00:09:23.771
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:23.771 --> 00:09:29.080
[MUSIC]

